<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonio de Mendoza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1495">1495</a>–<a href="/years/1552">1552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonio de Mendoza was a Castilian noble who became the first viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> in 1535. He set up the viceregal administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, founded the first mint in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and encouraged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> and education. He sent expeditions north, including that of Francisco Vázquez de Coronado, and later became viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza.jpg" alt="Antonio de Mendoza" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AntonioMendoza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antonio de Mendoza was born in Alcalá la Real, the second son of Íñigo López de Mendoza y Quiñones, 2nd Count of Tendilla, and his second wife Francisca Pacheco Portocarrero, of the House of Mendoza, one of the leading noble families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. He was a Knight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> and represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corte">Cortes</a> of Valladolid in 1518. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolt-of-the-comuneros">Revolt of the Comuneros</a> he supported the Crown, then entered the diplomatic service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, serving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Selected in 1529 as the first viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, Mendoza was named in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> on 17 April 1535 and governed for fifteen years, longer than any later viceroy. He found a recently conquered land beset by Indigenous unrest and rivalry among the Spaniards, and had to govern in the king's name without making an enemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>. With Bishop Juan de Zumárraga he helped found the Colegio de Santa Cruz at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> in 1536 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-and-pontifical-university-of-mexico">Royal and Pontifical University of Mexico</a> in 1552. In 1536 he began the minting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins, and in 1539 the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press in the New World reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> under his instructions.</p><p>When the crown issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws</a> restricting the grants of labour to conquerors, he declined to enforce their harshest parts, with the policy of obedezco pero no cumplo, &quot;I obey but do not comply&quot;. In 1542 he took the field against the Mixtón Rebellion, after which men, women and children were seized and executed, some by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> fire or by dogs. He commissioned Francisco Vásquez de Coronado's expedition to the northern lands in 1540 to 1542, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-mendoza">Codex Mendoza</a> was made at his order.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Named viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> on 4 July 1549, Mendoza travelled overland from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> and then by boat to Peru, but soon fell ill and died on 21 July 1552. His tomb is in the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, beside that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a>. He is credited with consolidating the Crown's sovereignty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> and limiting the power of the first conquistadors, and many of his policies lasted through the colonial period. Cape Mendocino in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> was named for him in 1565.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antonio de Mendoza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It created the Audiencia of Mexico, a royal court and governing council, in 1527, and in 1535 appointed <b>Antonio de Mendoza</b> as the first viceroy, who governed until 1550 and laid the foundations of colonial administration, founding the Mexico City mint and supporting the first printing press in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its early history is known chiefly from the Relación de Michoacán, compiled around 1539–1541, probably by the Franciscan friar Jerónimo de Alcalá, for Viceroy <b>Antonio de Mendoza</b> from the testimony of Purépecha nobles and priests.</blockquote><a href="/empire/purepecha-empire">Purépecha Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1532" aria-label="Granada on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4460 825 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antonio de Mendoza"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4910 1095h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4910 1095h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.18,-3.6&amp;year=1532">What was here in 1532? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
